(160727, 8776, 674, '<td valign="top">Critique club:<br/>1) COMPOSITION -CONTENT -  I would have focused more on the child, the brown dog is appearing as the focal point of the image. I wonder what the image would have looked like if composed from the left hand side?<br/>(2) BACKGROUND -  A very neutral background which neither adds to or subtracts from the main image<br/>(3) CAMERA WORK -TECHNICAL -   I feel that the most in focus part of the image is the piece of wall behind the brown dog perhaps the camera locked on to this point<br/><br/>(4) DIGITAL PROCESSING - TECHNICAL - The image has been processed correctly, although perhaps needed some unsharp mask applied after resizing!<br/><br/>(5) YOUR OPINION ON THE PHOTO -Thishave more emotional impact if I didn''t get the impression that the child is to clean and the dogs too well fed!<br/>It would be a topical story,for a real photojournalism article that makes a statement, about the plight of the street children of South Africa . I feel this idea had all the components to be a wow image but didn''t quite make it! I voted it an eight in the challenge</td>', 'Critique club:1) COMPOSITION -CONTENT -  I would have focused more on the child, the brown dog is appearing as the focal point of the image. I wonder what the image would have looked like if composed from the left hand side?(2) BACKGROUND -  A very neutral background which neither adds to or subtracts from the main image(3) CAMERA WORK -TECHNICAL -   I feel that the most in focus part of the image is the piece of wall behind the brown dog perhaps the camera locked on to this point(4) DIGITAL PROCESSING - TECHNICAL - The image has been processed correctly, although perhaps needed some unsharp mask applied after resizing!(5) YOUR OPINION ON THE PHOTO -Thishave more emotional impact if I didn''t get the impression that the child is to clean and the dogs too well fed!It would be a topical story,for a real photojournalism article that makes a statement, about the plight of the street children of South Africa . I feel this idea had all the components to be a wow image but didn''t quite make it! I voted it an eight in the challenge', '2002-12-02 02:44:00.000000', NULL, 0),
(160728, 8776, 1019, '<td valign="top">Thank you for all your kind comments on my picturethere is a story behind this photo and I thought you might be interested in it. I first saw this Street child about three weeks ago while I was in the car waiting for my wifeso I had lots of time to study him and the group he was withabout 4 adults and 2 children. They sleep literally on the pavement and store their mattresses and blankets in a portable garbage bin with wheels on. They wash  in a house sort of a squat filled with Nigerian and Congolese refugeesbut for a squat it looks good and cleanand the street is clean. I was stunned when the boy got a cigarette and started smoking it while playing with his skateboard and with the white dog. By this time I was cursing that I didnt have my camera and I was determined to return. This I did and I started chatting to him and gave him some money for a photothis is my first and I think my best one. About a week later the Challenge was photo-journalism and I thought I just had to get a picture of the little guy. They were all still there and another dog so I paid him money to pose for me and the brown dog insisted on sitting in with ushe would not be budgedI was warned that if I tried to move him he would bite me! That is why the brown dog is in the photo and why it was such a difficult crop and composition. The boy? his future seems fairly bleak. If you ask him if he is happyyes he ishe has a great time skateboarding and playinghow long that lasts and he starts begging I dont knowhe is not at school and will get no educationbut he is a bright boy and if someone took him under their wing he would turn out well or have a chanceI found it quite a sobering and worrying encounter.</td>', 'Thank you for all your kind comments on my picturethere is a story behind this photo and I thought you might be interested in it. I first saw this Street child about three weeks ago while I was in the car waiting for my wifeso I had lots of time to study him and the group he was withabout 4 adults and 2 children. They sleep literally on the pavement and store their mattresses and blankets in a portable garbage bin with wheels on. They wash  in a house sort of a squat filled with Nigerian and Congolese refugeesbut for a squat it looks good and cleanand the street is clean. I was stunned when the boy got a cigarette and started smoking it while playing with his skateboard and with the white dog. By this time I was cursing that I didnt have my camera and I was determined to return. This I did and I started chatting to him and gave him some money for a photothis is my first and I think my best one. About a week later the Challenge was photo-journalism and I thought I just had to get a picture of the little guy. They were all still there and another dog so I paid him money to pose for me and the brown dog insisted on sitting in with ushe would not be budgedI was warned that if I tried to move him he would bite me! That is why the brown dog is in the photo and why it was such a difficult crop and composition. The boy? his future seems fairly bleak. If you ask him if he is happyyes he ishe has a great time skateboarding and playinghow long that lasts and he starts begging I dont knowhe is not at school and will get no educationbut he is a bright boy and if someone took him under their wing he would turn out well or have a chanceI found it quite a sobering and worrying encounter.', '2002-12-02 09:52:00.000000', NULL, 0),
